{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And behold, the staff of Aaron blossomed, etc.</b> This is a hint that HaShem does not judge by the sight of human eyes: for if one appears to be a dry stick, without any moisture of Torah and wisdom, the Holy One, blessed be He, makes it blossom — and so too the reverse, as is concluded in the Yalkut (on Ezekiel 17, see there), which expounded concerning Korah and Aaron the verse ״הוֹבַשְׁתִּי עֵץ לָח וְהִפְרַחְתִּי עֵץ יָבֵשׁ״ (Ezekiel 17:24). And it may be that from there derives the source that everything that was in the Sanctuary would put forth leaves and produce fruit — even the gold, which is called פַּרְוַיִם. 'וַיּוֹצֵא פֶרַח' — 'and it produced a blossom' — this was a sign for the minor priests, who are called פִּרְחֵי כְּהֻנָּה, 'blossoms of the priesthood.' 'וַיָּצֵץ צִיץ' — 'and it put forth a bud' — this is a sign for the High Priisors who bear צִיץ נֵזֶר הַקֹּדֶשׁ, the holy crown of consecration. 'וַיִּגְמֹל שְׁקֵדִים' — 'and it yielded almonds' — in the merit of ״וַיָּרָץ אֶל תּוֹךְ הַקָּהָל״, etc., to halt the plague, and in the merit that priests are zealous, he was repaid measure for measure by the sprouting of שְׁקֵדִים, almonds, which carry the connotation of שְׁקִידוּת וּמְהִירוּת, diligence and swiftness. And he merited the gifts, as it says: ״וַאֲנִי הִנֵּה נָתַתִּי לְךָ״, and the expression הִנֵּה indicates zeal. And thus Rashi explained in parashat Vayeshev (Genesis 37:13): ״וַיֹּאמֶר הִנֵּנִי״ — an expression of humility and zeal. And Rashi explained here: הִנֵּה — with joy, as it is stated: ״הִנֵּה הוּא יוֹצֵא לִקְרָאתֶךָ וְרָאֲךָ וְשָׂמַח בְּלִבּוֹ״ (Exodus 4:14). And the reason for this is that every הִנֵּה is an expression of zeal, and whatever a person does with zeal — he is undoubtedly joyful in his doing. And just as Aaron was joyful to meet Moses his younger brother and was not jealous of his authority, by virtue of this Aaron was given the prerogative of the priesthood, to be anointed in it with joy — this is what it says: ״וַאֲנִי הִנֵּה נָתַתִּי לְךָ״, in the merit of ״הִנֵּה הוּא יוֹצֵא לִקְרָאתֶךָ״. And above, in the verse ״בֹּקֶר וְיוֹדַע ה׳״ — HaShem gave him the gifts in the morning, with a radiant countenance, in joy, as is fitting for anything given to the one who asks. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Shabbat 139a) that in the merit of ״וְרָאֲךָ וְשָׂמַח בְּלִבּוֹ״, he merited the חֹשֶׁן מִשְׁפָּט, the breastplate of judgment, upon the heart, as it is written (Psalms 25:9): ״יַדְרֵךְ עֲנָוִים בַּמִּשְׁפָּט״, as was explained above in parashat Mishpatim (Exodus 21:1), and this — rejoicing over his brother's authority — was humility on his part. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ḥullin 89a): Great is what is said of Moses and Aaron, as it is written (Exodus 16:8): ״וְנַחְנוּ מָה״, and likewise of Aaron it is written (Numbers 16:12): ״וְאַהֲרֹן מַה הוּא״ — for he reckons himself as nothing, as though he were a dry stick that has no moisture at all; therefore he merited that the staff of Aaron, though dry, blossomed." "And the fact that twenty-four gifts were given to him corresponds to the twenty-four books, as Rabbi Shmuel bar Naḥmani said in the name of Rabbi Yonatan (Sanhedrin 90b): From where do we derive that gifts are not given to an ignorant priest? As it is written (II Chronicles 31:4): ״לָתֵת מְנָת הַכֹּהֲנִים וְהַלְוִיִּם לְמַעַן יֶחֱזְקוּ בְּתוֹרַת ה׳״, etc. And in the Yalkut (section 799, see there) it counts twenty-four gifts — twelve in the Sanctuary and twelve in the outlying districts — alluding to the matter: ״זֶה יִהְיֶה לְךָ מִקֹּדֶשׁ הַקֳּדָשִׁים״, corresponding to the numerical value of זֶה, which is twelve — 'shall be yours from the most holy' refers to the twelve that are in the Sanctuary; ״וְזֶה לְךָ תְּרוּמַת מַתָּנָם לְכָל תְּנוּפוֹת בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — this corresponds to the twelve in the outlying districts, for there is no Sanctuary there. And above, in parashat Nasso (Numbers 6:23), we explained that there is yet another form of the service of a gift, and that is the Priestly Blessing — ״כֹּה תְבָרֲכוּ״ — for through it they will be twenty-five, and here it is alluded to by the extra expression הִנֵּה (Numbers 18:8), which yields sixty, corresponding to the number ״שִׁשִּׁים גִּבֹּרִים סָבִיב לָהּ״ (Song of Songs 3:7), which are alluded to in the Priestly Blessing; and there, radiant countenances are stated: ״יָאֵר ה׳ פָּנָיו אֵלֶיךָ״, and that is the meaning of הִנֵּה — with joy."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "Minor priests (כֹּהֲנִים קְטַנִּים): preserved the author's term, referring to ordinary priests as distinguished from the High Priest. The gematria of הִנֵּה yielding sixty is not explicitly spelled out by the author here but is presumed; the second comment (index 1) makes the calculation explicit."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:10.175268147Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}